1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,075
Here's a sentence you'll hear in a real meeting: "the model's fine, it's a retrieval

2
00:00:05,075 --> 00:00:09,519
problem." If you don't know there's a difference between those two things, that

3
00:00:09,519 --> 00:00:12,275
sentence just ended the conversation without you.

4
00:00:12,275 --> 00:00:16,832
There are really five layers in any AI feature, stacked on top of each other, and

5
00:00:16,832 --> 00:00:21,444
almost every technical disagreement you'll sit through is actually about which one

6
00:00:21,444 --> 00:00:24,257
of the five is broken, or which one should change.

7
00:00:24,257 --> 00:00:28,307
Learn this diagram well enough to redraw it, and you stop nodding along.

8
00:00:28,307 --> 00:00:32,751
You start asking the one question that actually moves the conversation forward:

9
00:00:32,751 --> 00:00:34,607
which layer are we talking about?

10
00:00:34,607 --> 00:00:38,545
This is also the fastest credibility you'll earn in this whole course.

11
00:00:38,545 --> 00:00:41,132
Engineers don't expect a PM to know the stack.

12
00:00:41,132 --> 00:00:45,801
Asking "is that a retrieval issue or an orchestration issue" the first time changes

13
00:00:45,801 --> 00:00:47,939
how the rest of the conversation goes.

14
00:00:48,489 --> 00:00:53,119
Here's the whole thing, top to bottom, the way a user's request actually travels.

15
00:00:53,119 --> 00:00:57,635
Your product is what the user sees, the chat window, the button, the summary on

16
00:00:57,635 --> 00:00:58,035
screen.

17
00:00:58,035 --> 00:01:02,493
Guardrails sit just underneath, checking what goes in and what comes back out.

18
00:01:02,493 --> 00:01:07,295
Orchestration is the layer most PMs have never heard named, even though they've been

19
00:01:07,295 --> 00:01:12,097
debugging it for months: the code that assembles the prompt, decides what context to

20
00:01:12,097 --> 00:01:15,183
include, and calls everything else in the right order.

21
00:01:15,183 --> 00:01:18,384
That orchestration layer talks to two things beneath it.

22
00:01:18,384 --> 00:01:21,414
The foundation model, the mechanism from Section one.

23
00:01:21,414 --> 00:01:25,987
And your data, whatever documents or records get retrieved to ground the answer.

24
00:01:25,987 --> 00:01:30,732
Almost every bug you've ever blamed on "the model" actually lived in orchestration:

25
00:01:30,732 --> 00:01:35,419
the wrong document got pulled, the prompt assembled the pieces in the wrong order,

26
00:01:35,419 --> 00:01:38,448
the context window ran out before the important part.

27
00:01:38,448 --> 00:01:42,850
Follow the arrows back up and you get the return trip: the model answers, the

28
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:46,851
guardrails check it again on the way out, and the product displays it.

29
00:01:46,851 --> 00:01:49,938
Same five layers, same order, just running in reverse.

30
00:01:50,488 --> 00:01:55,475
Here's why naming the layer matters practically: each one has a different owner and

31
00:01:55,475 --> 00:01:56,437
a different fix.

32
00:01:56,437 --> 00:02:01,304
A product problem is yours, confusing UI, the feature answering a question nobody

33
00:02:01,304 --> 00:02:02,205
actually asked.

34
00:02:02,205 --> 00:02:07,012
A guardrail problem sits with whoever owns risk, something unsafe got through or

35
00:02:07,012 --> 00:02:10,197
something safe got blocked by an overcautious filter.

36
00:02:10,197 --> 00:02:15,004
Orchestration problems are engineering's, and they're the most common bug you'll

37
00:02:15,004 --> 00:02:19,330
actually hit: the wrong document got retrieved, the prompt assembled the

38
00:02:19,330 --> 00:02:21,733
conversation history in the wrong order.

39
00:02:21,733 --> 00:02:26,781
A genuine foundation model limit is the one thing you can't patch, only route around

40
00:02:26,781 --> 00:02:27,502
or wait out.

41
00:02:27,502 --> 00:02:32,249
And a data problem sits with whoever owns the content: stale documents, missing

42
00:02:32,249 --> 00:02:33,811
pages, badly chunked text.

43
00:02:33,811 --> 00:02:37,837
Five different people, five different Monday morning conversations.

44
00:02:37,837 --> 00:02:42,704
Most launch-week arguments are actually two people disagreeing about which row of

45
00:02:42,704 --> 00:02:46,369
this table they're in, without either one saying so out loud.

46
00:02:46,919 --> 00:02:51,864
Say the honest caveat, because this diagram can look like five separate teams and

47
00:02:51,864 --> 00:02:52,597
often isn't.

48
00:02:52,597 --> 00:02:57,725
On a small team, or for a simple feature, one engineer might write the orchestration

49
00:02:57,725 --> 00:03:02,853
code, choose the guardrails, and pick the data source, all in the same afternoon, in

50
00:03:02,853 --> 00:03:03,708
the same file.

51
00:03:03,708 --> 00:03:05,417
The five layers still exist.

52
00:03:05,417 --> 00:03:07,981
They're just not five separate people yet.

53
00:03:07,981 --> 00:03:13,048
That's exactly when bugs hide, because nobody's drawing the line between "this is a

54
00:03:13,048 --> 00:03:16,833
data problem" and "this is an orchestration problem" out loud.

55
00:03:16,833 --> 00:03:21,778
Naming the layer is useful precisely because it forces that distinction even when

56
00:03:21,778 --> 00:03:23,671
one person wears all five hats.

57
00:03:24,221 --> 00:03:28,981
That's the entire practical payoff of this diagram: one question, asked before the

58
00:03:28,981 --> 00:03:32,174
debugging starts, instead of after an hour of guessing.

59
00:03:32,174 --> 00:03:35,076
The exercise is just that question, used for real.

60
00:03:35,076 --> 00:03:39,430
Next meeting where something's "not working," ask which of the five layers.

61
00:03:39,430 --> 00:03:44,016
You'll be surprised how often the room doesn't actually agree yet, and how much

62
00:03:44,016 --> 00:03:45,874
faster things move once it does.

63
00:03:46,424 --> 00:03:48,139
One thing before you move on.

64
00:03:48,139 --> 00:03:52,634
Pick whatever's currently broken or annoying about your feature, and settle,

65
00:03:52,634 --> 00:03:55,413
specifically, which of the five layers owns it.

66
00:03:55,413 --> 00:03:56,123
Not a guess.

67
00:03:56,123 --> 00:03:58,547
If nothing comes easily, reread that one.

68
00:03:58,547 --> 00:04:03,397
Next lecture uses this exact stack to answer the question everyone asks too early:

69
00:04:03,397 --> 00:04:08,305
prompt, retrieval, fine-tune, or agent, and the decision tree that actually settles

70
00:04:08,305 --> 00:04:08,483
it.
